<body>

<h1>Providence E-Commerce Marketplace: Project Guide</h1>
<p>This guide is written in clear, simple language with relatable analogies. It is designed to help you understand what the project does, how it works conceptually, and why each module is structured the way it is.</p>

<hr>

<h2>1. The Big Picture: What is this project?</h2>
<p>Think of this project as a <strong>Digital Flea Market (E-Commerce Store)</strong>.</p>
<ul>
    <li>Normal users (<strong>Clients</strong>) can browse products, search for items by name or city, add items to a shopping cart, and "buy" them using a simulated credit card. They can also register as sellers and list their own items for sale.</li>
    <li>Special users (<strong>Admins</strong>) can access a back-office control desk to view total store sales, see a list of accounts, and suspend any user who acts suspiciously.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>2. Core Concepts: The Two Halves of the App</h2>
<p>A full-stack application is like a <strong>Restaurant</strong>:</p>

<div class="analogy">
    <strong>Restaurant Analogy:</strong><br>
    <strong>1. The Frontend (Dining Room):</strong> This is what the user sees on their screen. It is built with React. It displays the buttons, forms, and product grids. It doesn't actually store products or check passwords; it just collects user inputs and sends them to the back.<br>
    <strong>2. The Backend (Kitchen):</strong> This is the server running behind the scenes. It is built with Node.js and Express.js. It takes requests from the dining room (frontend), runs security checks, calculates prices, and talks to the database pantry.
</div>

<hr>

<h2>3. The Database: Where Data Lives</h2>
<p>The database is like a <strong>Digital File Cabinet</strong>. In our project, we use MongoDB (with Mongoose to interact with it).</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>MongoDB:</strong> A database that stores information in flexible, text-like documents (JSON format) instead of strict tables with rows and columns.</li>
    <li><strong>Schema:</strong> A blueprint or blank template form. For example, the Product Schema dictates that every product folder must contain a title (text), a price (number), a category (text), and a sellerId (text). This keeps our file cabinet organized.</li>
    <li><strong>Model Proxy (modelFactory.js):</strong> Think of this as a Middleman Clerk. When a controller wants to find a file, it asks the Proxy Clerk. The Proxy Clerk creates the Mongoose schema and interacts with the database on behalf of the controller.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>4. Security, Wristbands, and Guards (Authentication & Authorization)</h2>
<p>How does the server know who you are and prevent random people from deleting your products?</p>

<h3>A. Password Hashing (Bcrypt)</h3>
<p>If you write plain passwords in a database (like "secret123"), and someone hacks the database, all user accounts are stolen. We use Bcrypt to resolve this.</p>
<div class="analogy">
    <strong>Blender Analogy:</strong><br>
    Think of Bcrypt as a food blender. When you sign up, Bcrypt takes your password, runs it through a blender with random data (called a "salt"), and creates a scrambled message (like "$2a$10$X87..."). When you log in, Bcrypt blends your inputted password and checks if the scrambled results match. The server never stores or knows your actual plain-text password.
</div>

<h3>B. JWT Tokens (JSON Web Tokens)</h3>
<p>How does the app remember you're logged in?</p>
<div class="analogy">
    <strong>Festival Wristband Analogy:</strong><br>
    Think of a JWT token as a wristband at a music festival. When you type your correct email and password, the server hands you a signed, encrypted wristband (the JWT token). The next time you want to view your cart or list a product, you don't type your password again. Your browser simply shows the server your digital wristband. The server checks the signature and says, "Ah, this is Alen. Welcome back!"
</div>

<h3>C. Middlewares (Security Guards)</h3>
<p>Express uses "middlewares" (like <code>protect</code>) to secure private endpoints.</p>
<div class="analogy">
    <strong>VIP Door Guard Analogy:</strong><br>
    When you send a request to a private page, it hits the security guard (middleware) first:
    <ol>
        <li>The guard checks if you have a wristband (token).</li>
        <li>The guard decodes it to see your User ID.</li>
        <li>The guard checks the database to see if your account was suspended by an administrator. If suspended, the guard stops you immediately and throws you out (403 Forbidden).</li>
        <li>If all checks pass, the guard opens the door (calls next()) and lets the server process your request.</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<hr>

<h2>5. How Searching Works: Dynamic Regex Search</h2>
<p>When a user types "phone" in the search bar, how does the backend find it?</p>
<div class="analogy">
    <strong>Librarian Analogy:</strong><br>
    Think of it as a librarian looking for books containing parts of a word. We use a database tool called Regular Expressions ($regex) with the case-insensitive option ('i'). If you search for "IPHONE", the database searches for any match containing those letters in any case (matching "iphone", "iPhone", "Iphone").
</div>

<hr>

<h2>6. The Checkout & The "0000" Payment Card Trick</h2>
<p>We simulated a complete checkout flow:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Cart Total:</strong> The server sums up the prices of all items in your cart. If the subtotal is under $150, it adds a $15 shipping fee; otherwise, shipping is free.</li>
    <li><strong>Payment Validation:</strong> The server checks that you entered a valid-looking card number (15 or 16 digits) and a CVV (3 or 4 digits).</li>
    <li><strong>The "0000" Trick:</strong> Since we don't have a real Visa/Mastercard processing bank attached, we coded a rule:
        <ul>
            <li>If a card number ends in "0000", the server acts as if the bank declined the transaction. It returns a 402 Payment Required error.</li>
            <li>Any other card number succeeds. The order status is set to Paid and saved.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>7. The Administrator's Dashboard</h2>
<p>Admins have access to a master control desk:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Calculated Revenue:</strong> The server finds all orders in the database marked as Paid. It uses a loop (.reduce()) to sum up their total values and displays the grand revenue total on the dashboard.</li>
    <li><strong>Account Suspension Toggle:</strong> If an admin clicks "Suspend" next to a user's name:
        <ul>
            <li>The server changes that user's isSuspended database value to true.</li>
            <li>The next time that user tries to make any API call, the protect security guard checks their profile, sees isSuspended: true, and immediately blocks them from the app.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>8. Frontend Mechanics: How the UI Reacts</h2>
<p>The frontend is built with React to make it feel fast, smooth, and modern.</p>

<h3>A. React & SPA (Single Page Application)</h3>
<p>In old websites, clicking a link meant the screen went blank and reloaded a completely new page from scratch. In a React SPA, you never download a new page. The browser downloads the app once. When you click "Cart", React instantly deletes the product grid block from the screen and draws the cart block in its place. This happens in milliseconds and makes the app feel like a desktop application.</p>

<h3>B. React Context API (AppContext.jsx)</h3>
<div class="analogy">
    <strong>Central Bulletin Board Analogy:</strong><br>
    Normally, components in React cannot easily share information (e.g., the Header needs to know how many items are in your Shopping Cart, but the Cart is managed on the Product page). AppContext acts as a central board that holds the user session state and the cart list. Any page or button on the website can subscribe to the board to read the cart items or update the login state instantly.
</div>

<h3>C. Local Storage Synchronization</h3>
<div class="analogy">
    <strong>Sticky Notepad Analogy:</strong><br>
    If you close the browser tab or refresh the page, normal React state variables are erased. To prevent this, every time you add or remove items from the cart, a React hook (useEffect) saves a copy to the browser's local storage notepad. When the page first loads, React checks this notepad and loads your previous cart back into memory.
</div>

<hr>

<h2>9. Common Interview Q&As to Ace Your Presentation</h2>

<h3>Q1: Why did you use React Context instead of standard state passing?</h3>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> "We used React Context (AppContext.jsx) to establish a global state for the logged-in user and the shopping cart. Passing state down through multiple levels of components (prop-drilling) makes code hard to maintain. Context allows elements like the Navbar cart badge, the Catalog, and the Checkout page to access and update the same state data in real-time."</p>

<h3>Q2: How does security work on your backend APIs?</h3>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> "Security is structured around stateless authentication using JWT. When users log in, they receive a signed JWT token. The frontend includes this token in the headers of all secure requests. A custom Express middleware (protect) intercepts these requests, verifies the token's cryptographic signature, and checks the user's suspension status in the database before allowing the controller to run."</p>

<h3>Q3: How is the search and filtering implemented on the server?</h3>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> "In productController.js, filters are parsed dynamically from the query string parameters. We construct a query object utilizing MongoDB regex options ($regex and $options: 'i'). This matches user search inputs case-insensitively across product titles and descriptions, allowing for flexible searches."</p>

<h3>Q4: How is the frontend and backend launched together?</h3>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> "We configured a root-level package.json utilizing the concurrently module. Running npm run dev launches both the Node/Express backend on port 5000 and the Vite/React frontend server on port 5173 simultaneously from a single terminal console."</p>

